  "reasoning": "Genuine bug fix for a deadlock in Container.WaitOnStopTimeout: once StopLoopForContainer times out and jumps to the kill loop, nothing consumes stopTimeoutChan anymore, yet WaitOnStopTimeout (called with c.stopLock held) does a blocking send on that 10-buffered channel - once the buffer fills, every caller deadlocks while holding the container's stopLock. The fix adds a stopKillLoopBegun flag, a SetStopKillLoopBegun() setter, converts the blocking send into a select/default non-blocking send gated on the flag, and calls the setter from StopLoopForContainer right before 'goto killContainer', plus two debug log lines. No test files are included in the commit; the existing internal/oci/runtime_oci_test.go StopLoopForContainer specs ('should fall back to KILL after timeout', 'should interrupt longer stop timeout', 'should handle many updates') already exercise this exact code path and pass on the target today, so they serve as the regression suite. A dry-run cherry-pick onto target produced exactly 3 conflict regions, all mechanical: (1) container.go Container struct field block - source renamed imageName to someNameOfTheImage and realigned; resolution must keep target's imageName field name and alignment while adding the new comment block and the 'stopKillLoopBegun bool' field, then re-run gofmt because the struct tags must be realigned (gofmt/golangci-lint gofmt would flag it); (2) container.go NewContainer struct literal - keep target's 'imageName: imageName,' and add 'stopKillLoopBegun: false,' (zero value; may also be omitted) with correct alignment; (3) runtime_oci.go stop-timeout Warnf line - keep either message variant (c.GetStopSignal() exists on target) but MUST retain the new 'c.SetStopKillLoopBegun()' call before 'goto killContainer'. The critical hunk - the new SetStopKillLoopBegun method and the select/default channel logic in WaitOnStopTimeout - auto-merges cleanly with no manual work. Do NOT port the imageName->someNameOfTheImage rename or the context import reorder from the source side of the conflicts; the agent should adapt to target's existing API. Risk is moderate: the conflicts are tiny and localized to two files, but mis-resolving the struct block (e.g. taking the source side's field rename) would break many call sites across the codebase, hence PROCEED_WITH_CAUTION rather than PROCEED. Difficulty MEDIUM: new method/field on an existing type plus guaranteed content conflicts, but zero prerequisites, zero new packages, zero generated files, and a passing in-tree test suite that directly covers the fixed path (verified: ./internal/oci tests pass on unmodified target in ~30s, and go build/go vet of the package succeed)."
}
